      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=8 x^{2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=16 x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{16}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 16 x \\cos{\\left(8 x^{2} \\right)}\\,\\text{d}x &=\\int 16 \\cos{\\left(8 x^{2} \\right)}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ &=16\\int \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &=1\\int \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &=\\sin{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\ &=\\sin{\\left(8 x^{2} \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy"
