            {
              ":answer/id": "e0fe0e90-1c93-5759-bca8-8bb0b64211d8",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "\\frac{9}{10}"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "e0fe0e90-1c93-5759-bca8-8bb0b64211d8"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "If we let $u=5 x^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=10 x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{10}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{15} \\sqrt{\\pi}}{15}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{15} \\sqrt{\\pi}}{15}} 9 x \\tan{\\left(5 x^{2} \\right)} \\sec{\\left(5 x^{2} \\right)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{15} \\sqrt{\\pi}}{15}} 9 \\tan{\\left(5 x^{2} \\right)} \\sec{\\left(5 x^{2} \\right)}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}} \\tan{\\left(u \\right)} \\sec{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9 \\sec{\\left(u \\right)}}{10}\\big|_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}\\left(\\sec{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\right)}-\\left(\\sec{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}\\left(2-\\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard"
    },
