      ":question/worked-solution": "If we let $u=4 x^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8 x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{8}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\sqrt{\\pi}}{4}$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi}}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /2$ | $\\pi$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\sqrt{\\pi}}{4}}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi}}{2}} 2 x \\cos{\\left(4 x^{2} \\right)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int_{\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\sqrt{\\pi}}{4}}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi}}{2}} 2 \\cos{\\left(4 x^{2} \\right)}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi} \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sin{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}\\big|_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\sin{\\left(\\left(\\pi\\right) \\right)}-\\left(\\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left(0-\\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
